## Files

- `auto_splines_bench_2026-05-17.tsv` — consolidated. 3 synthetics × {e7, e8,
  e9} + 5 photos × {e8, e9} + 3 screenshots × {e8, e9} = 25 rows. Photos and
  screenshots only at e8/e9 (the flip target); synthetics at e7 too to
  baseline the chunk-3 detector's intended wins.

## Decision: SKIP THE FLIP

Acceptance criteria from W6-2 + W7-4 spec:
1. Photo no-regression at e8+: **HOLDS** (10/10 byte-identical)
2. Power-line wins persist at e8+: **FAILS** (3/3 synthetics 0 byte delta at e8+)

At e7 the chunk-3 detector behaves as designed:
| Image                     | off bytes | on bytes | delta   |
|---------------------------|-----------|----------|---------|
| synth_1line_1024x256      | 2318      | 2436     | +118 (cost) |
| synth_4lines_1024x512     | 4919      | 4781     | -138 (win) |
| synth_8lines_2048x1024    | 17924     | 17367    | -557 (win) |

At e8 and e9 every tested image is byte-identical (delta = +0 across the
board). Root cause is plausibly that the trial-encode cost gate
(`spline_passes_trial_encode_gate`) sees a smaller measured residual-energy
savings proxy on the buttloop-converged XYB and falls below the 2x
COST_BENEFIT_MARGIN, dropping every candidate. Diagnosing this further is
out of scope for the flip decision; the bench data alone is conclusive.

Flipping default-on at e8+ would pay the detector's CPU cost
(Sobel + NMS + Hessian + polyline trace + per-candidate trial encode) on
every default-effort-8/9 encode for **zero byte change**. Default stays
`false`. The infrastructure flip (auto_splines_explicit field,
`EffortProfile::auto_splines_default(effort)`) ships anyway — when the
detector evolves to win at e8+, only that one function needs updating.

## Hash locks

`cargo test --release --test hash_lock_features` → 36/36 pass.
`cargo test --release auto_splines` → 6/6 pass (incl. default_is_off,
chunk3_multi_line_decreases_bytes).
